                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Please don't forget to clean up commit message to match code changes."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: needs rephrasing"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "done please check"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you elaborate?  I mean it is clear that it is a classification but I'm not able to the conclusion on how it is helpful and/or useful.  I don't know of any use of this in Redfish (but maybe I'm missing it) so how is internal code going to leverage this for better decision making?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Redfish also has \u201creported\u201d, which I honestly can\u2019t figure out the difference of based on the wording. Do we need an \u201cunknown\u201d default also?  Otherwise you\u2019re going to end up with phosphor-virtual-sensors being \u201cPhysical\u201d by default which is not correct."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "Sensor schema on redfish does have the support for the ImplementationType describing whether reading exposed on the sensor uri belong to a physical hardware sensor or its synthesized one.\nLook for \"ImplementationType\"\nhttps://redfish.dmtf.org/schemas/v1/Sensor.v1_10_1.yaml"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Aishwary - I don't think you responded to all the questions. Please don't resolve until you have done that. I agree, we need an unknown default else virtual sensor implementation is forced to implement this.\n\nI also don't underatand the \"Reported\" enum in the RF schema (don't understand how it differs from synthesized/software provided), so I would say we don't add that."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Also what happened to PLDM?  Is this kind of information able to be derived?  Or maybe that\u2019s what \u201creported\u201d is for. \n\nI know the enum information as presented here is copied from Redfish. I\u2019d like it if we also document how they are intended to be used within OpenBMC.  Redfish is ambiguous; let\u2019s not be here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "Extend the enum to include Unknown.\nTo get the clarification \"Reported\" we already have a thread on DMTF\nhttps://redfishforum.com/thread/1165/enums-on-sensor-implementation-type\n\nHave added only two enums values for implementation type as those had clear definition hence included those two only.\n\nHave added unknown where classification is not possbible"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "The pldm service on the bmc is measuring one particular physical quantity(temperature, voltage, energy..) of the device bmc(local hardware) is directly monitoring.\n\nAs per discussion on redfish forum the \"Reported\" is when the BMC is not directly polling the device for fetching the sensor reading rather sensor readings are pushed from other software and sensor is being presented as on of the its own.\nExternalSensor in dbus-sensor daemon fits well for \"Reported\"."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "So a sensor type as \nPhysical: It means the service is monitoring the sensor reading of the local hardware component through its BMC.\n\nReported: It means the service is not directly/actively monitoring the sensor reading but relies on software to monitor the reading\n\nSynthesized: Readings calculated from other properties or sensors"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Value.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is this something that can even be determined with protocols like PLDM?\n\nWhen it says \"one or more\", I wonder how you can really differentiate this in some cases.  Many of the dbus-sensors have a \"scale\" factor or some simple calculation they do to clean up the hardware results.  Does this make it \"sythesized\"?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Sensor/Value.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "aishwaryjoshi31",
                        "email": "aishwaryjoshi31@gmail.com",
                        "username": "aishwaryjoshi31"
                    },
                    "message": "AFAIK scale factor would only be for unit conversion but that doesnt change the sensor reading provided on the sensor uri is for a specific hardware component(thermal/power/energy)\n\nExample, a sensor reporting the power drawn by a gpu will be a physical sensor to report the gpu power consumption, \nand in a system with more than one gpu, Total Power consumed by all gpus can be a synthesized sensor(computed based on reading from other sensors)"
                }
            ],
